1
00:00:02,443 --> 00:00:04,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we're going to go through Majjhima Nikaya No.

2
00:00:04,824 --> 00:00:09,845
[SPEAKER_00]: 2 now, which is a Sābhāsala Sutta, translated as All the Defilements.

3
00:00:19,567 --> 00:00:31,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Sutta begins by saying the Buddha was staying near Savatthi, in Jetha's Grove, and he addressed the monks saying, monks, I will teach you the explanation of the restraint

4
00:00:32,038 --> 00:00:33,219
[SPEAKER_00]: of all defilements.

5
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Listen and apply your mind well.

6
00:00:37,441 --> 00:00:39,583
[SPEAKER_00]: I will speak.

7
00:00:39,603 --> 00:00:40,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the Buddha continues.

8
00:00:41,624 --> 00:00:48,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Monks, I say that the ending of defilements is for the one who knows and sees, not for the one who does not know or see.

9
00:00:50,309 --> 00:00:51,710
[SPEAKER_00]: For one who knows and sees what?

10
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:55,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Attention through source and attention through not-source.

11
00:00:57,041 --> 00:01:00,063
[SPEAKER_00]: When you attend not through the source, defilements arise.

12
00:01:00,544 --> 00:01:01,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Once arisen, they grow.

13
00:01:02,505 --> 00:01:09,010
[SPEAKER_00]: When you attend through the source, defilements don't arise, and those that have already arisen are given up, dispelled.

14
00:01:10,091 --> 00:01:20,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Some defilements should be given up by seeing, some by restraint, some by using, and some by bearing, some by avoiding, and some by dispelling, and some by developing.

15
00:01:22,381 --> 00:01:23,802
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's go to the beginning of this.

16
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:32,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Where the Buddha says, monks, I say that the ending of defilements is for the one who knows and sees, not for the one who doesn't know or see.

17
00:01:33,674 --> 00:01:45,040
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this statement means that the teaching that's about to follow, that the instructions he's about to give, apply to somebody who already has the right view.

18
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:46,881
[SPEAKER_00]: So, at least the Sotāpanna.

19
00:01:47,962 --> 00:01:54,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the one who sees, knows and sees the way out of suffering, not the one who doesn't know and see way out of suffering.

20
00:01:54,861 --> 00:02:09,395
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, if a person without a right view were to then just try and do and bear things or develop things and all these other things that the Buddha will describe one should do in the Sutta, it simply won't take place.

21
00:02:10,194 --> 00:02:12,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're missing the prerequisite for it.

22
00:02:12,995 --> 00:02:18,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Prerequisite for it is, as it says, as it continues here, for one who knows and sees what.

23
00:02:18,557 --> 00:02:22,039
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is it that distinguishes a sotāpanna from a bhutujana?

24
00:02:22,199 --> 00:02:27,561
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that necessary prerequisite that then makes the rest of this sutta apply?

25
00:02:28,241 --> 00:02:31,903
[SPEAKER_00]: There is attention through the source and attention not through the source.

26
00:02:32,543 --> 00:02:38,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, as we, probably people that follow this channel, they already know the term, Pali term is yoniso manasikāra.

27
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,931
[SPEAKER_00]: And ayoniso manasikāra.

28
00:02:42,011 --> 00:02:47,354
[SPEAKER_00]: We translated that in the past as concurrent attention, root attention, attention with the source.

29
00:02:48,835 --> 00:02:55,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, yoniso manasikāra, it's like 50% of the right view.

30
00:02:55,698 --> 00:02:57,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way.

31
00:02:58,059 --> 00:03:04,662
[SPEAKER_00]: In the sutta where the Buddha describes that there are two factors for the rising of the right view.

32
00:03:06,110 --> 00:03:07,511
[SPEAKER_00]: One is utterance of another.

33
00:03:07,671 --> 00:03:10,392
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, one is correct instruction.

34
00:03:11,753 --> 00:03:13,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Another one is yoniso manasikāra.

35
00:03:13,694 --> 00:03:21,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could be talking to the Buddha himself, and he can be giving you the most correct, the most accurate instruction.

36
00:03:24,939 --> 00:03:28,540
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be understanding it, if yoniso manasikāra is not developed.

37
00:03:29,301 --> 00:03:29,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Now,

38
00:03:30,579 --> 00:03:33,822
[SPEAKER_00]: yoniso manasikāra... yoniso means womb, manasikāra means attention.

39
00:03:33,902 --> 00:03:35,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, womb attention.

40
00:03:35,924 --> 00:03:37,726
[SPEAKER_00]: The source, the origin, attention.

41
00:03:38,486 --> 00:03:42,450
[SPEAKER_00]: That has been translated in many different ways.

42
00:03:43,391 --> 00:03:55,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is... the common translation is like a wise reflection or wise attention, implying it's something a person can just do after some serious consideration.

43
00:03:56,114 --> 00:04:15,765
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can take it to mean that, but then you should ask yourself, okay, so, if I have yoniso manasikāra, according to the meaning of wise consideration, for example, so, I am wisely considering things, and I got the right instruction, all the suttas, all the nikāyas, why am I not having the right view?

44
00:04:16,005 --> 00:04:18,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Because these are the two only factors for the right view.

45
00:04:19,307 --> 00:04:24,431
[SPEAKER_00]: If yoniso manasikāra meets the right instruction, right view has to take place.

46
00:04:25,191 --> 00:04:39,882
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, if a person is honest, they would ask themselves... well, they would inquire further and recognize that, okay, so it's either not the right instruction, or perhaps, my understanding of the first factor of yoniso manasikāra, it's not adequate.

47
00:04:40,955 --> 00:04:41,715
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem.

48
00:04:41,735 --> 00:04:47,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, these things that we would often say, oh, it's wrong, it's a wrong translation and stuff.

49
00:04:47,396 --> 00:04:49,056
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that they're completely wrong.

50
00:04:49,196 --> 00:04:52,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's nothing wrong in saying yoniso manasikāra is wise consideration.

51
00:04:53,197 --> 00:04:56,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Because yoniso manasikāra implies wise consideration.

52
00:04:57,378 --> 00:05:05,659
[SPEAKER_00]: But if... if it's limited just to the concept of wise consideration, you won't look further.

53
00:05:05,699 --> 00:05:09,760
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't make it even more significant and profound.

54
00:05:10,999 --> 00:05:19,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is obviously necessary, because, as the Sutta says, as the other Suttas say, yoniso manasikāra, instruction of another, means the right view.

55
00:05:19,746 --> 00:05:20,987
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no third option there.

56
00:05:22,488 --> 00:05:30,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is no right view upon meeting the teaching, it's either because the teaching is either wrong, or you don't have yoniso manasikāra.

57
00:05:30,455 --> 00:05:37,621
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to revise and reconsider, ironically, what yoniso manasikāra means and how deep that goes.

58
00:05:38,884 --> 00:05:57,119
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the the insistence and what I was translating in the past, calling it concurrent attention, it's just... it's just really more like just to... to kind of snap people out of that slumber of taking for granted, oh, yeah, wise reflection, wise consideration, I got that, I understand that, while at the same time knowing that they don't have the right view.

59
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:06,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you say it's a concurrent attention or attention with the source, it's kind of... it's confusing and that's already a good starting point.

60
00:06:07,524 --> 00:06:09,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you won't be taking it for granted.

61
00:06:09,185 --> 00:06:13,967
[SPEAKER_00]: You will take it as something that needs to be clarified and understood.

62
00:06:14,727 --> 00:06:21,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, attention through the source, in this translation that we chose, and attention not through the source.

63
00:06:22,450 --> 00:06:28,093
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might wonder then, what source?

64
00:06:28,653 --> 00:06:31,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it like some sort of mystical source?

65
00:06:31,394 --> 00:06:32,875
[SPEAKER_00]: What source does that refer to?

66
00:06:32,935 --> 00:06:33,335
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

67
00:06:34,831 --> 00:06:35,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, think about it.

68
00:06:35,791 --> 00:06:42,713
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, we did a whole, like, I don't know, how many talks on yoniso manasikāra, and we can probably link them here in the description.

69
00:06:44,174 --> 00:06:55,777
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people should, like, if they want to understand it, or get a bigger, wider picture on the indication of what it actually means, they should definitely listen to those talks.

70
00:06:55,877 --> 00:06:56,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

71
00:06:57,345 --> 00:07:23,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in brief here now, without going into... dedicating this whole talk just to the discussion of yoniso, attention with the source, or through the source, even better, again, implies the simultaneous presence of, say, that which you're attending, whatever that might be, particular, general, internal, external, personal, not personal, doesn't matter, whatever your current mind, currently your mind is occupied with, attending at, attending to,

72
00:07:27,079 --> 00:07:32,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneous to that, there is a simultaneous presence of a necessary basis for that.

73
00:07:33,986 --> 00:07:42,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever you were doing, whatever action you are engaged with, like, your attention naturally goes with that direction and and falls onto

74
00:07:43,959 --> 00:07:44,919
[SPEAKER_00]: What you are doing.

75
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:47,380
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the content of what you're doing.

76
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:57,564
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, like, the sensuality is one of the chief examples of ayoniso manasikāra, of attending not through the source, or not with the source, simultaneously recognized there.

77
00:07:58,824 --> 00:08:11,089
[SPEAKER_00]: You get overly concerned, overly invested, overly concentrated onto the objects of your senses, as opposed to not losing the sight,

78
00:08:12,082 --> 00:08:29,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Not losing the context, the perspective of whatever you are attending through your senses, the necessary basis, the source of that whole attending to this particular thing that arose on account of my sight, the necessary basis is simultaneously present there as a necessary basis.

79
00:08:30,235 --> 00:08:37,039
[SPEAKER_00]: If that basis is removed, if that source is taken away, no experience standing upon it can remain.

80
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:40,422
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is a recognition that Putujana can develop.

81
00:08:40,948 --> 00:08:46,390
[SPEAKER_00]: It can develop if he goes in that direction of yoniso, which obviously he has to, if he wants to get the right view.

82
00:08:46,970 --> 00:09:09,539
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a more practical example, the source is like... well, you could say, for example, if it's the central object you're looking at, the necessary source that's simultaneously present there, you could describe it as it's the necessity of the body, more specifically, necessity of the eye organs,

83
00:09:10,239 --> 00:09:14,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the basis, the field on which these things can be discerned.

84
00:09:15,302 --> 00:09:16,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, people forget that.

85
00:09:16,183 --> 00:09:22,167
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't think about their eyes when they're preoccupied with a lustful sight, or even disagreeable sight.

86
00:09:22,507 --> 00:09:26,229
[SPEAKER_00]: They look past the experience right here.

87
00:09:27,150 --> 00:09:35,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, when you're experiencing sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, you're experiencing your senses experiencing sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

88
00:09:36,115 --> 00:09:39,798
[SPEAKER_00]: But those senses, as an underlying basis, are overlooked.

89
00:09:41,007 --> 00:09:43,707
[SPEAKER_00]: People see sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches directly.

90
00:09:43,787 --> 00:09:49,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Or they assume it's a direct connection, direct perception of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches over there in the world.

91
00:09:49,769 --> 00:09:57,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, really, the closest to the direct connection is just you being aware of what your senses are perceiving without seeing the senses.

92
00:09:58,690 --> 00:09:59,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Overlooking the source.

93
00:10:00,570 --> 00:10:06,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you attend to the sense objects, you don't attend through the necessary basis of those sense objects.

94
00:10:07,272 --> 00:10:08,112
[SPEAKER_00]: You completely ignore it.

95
00:10:08,132 --> 00:10:09,192
[SPEAKER_00]: You're unaware of it.

96
00:10:09,852 --> 00:10:10,472
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

97
00:10:11,013 --> 00:10:14,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's discerning that which is seemingly invisible.

98
00:10:15,275 --> 00:10:20,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Your eye is invisible in your sights, yet is a necessary basis for sights to exist.

99
00:10:21,399 --> 00:10:28,624
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not losing the context of the necessity of the eyes when you are looking at the sights is already close to yoniso.

100
00:10:29,344 --> 00:10:30,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Yoniso manasikāra.

101
00:10:30,605 --> 00:10:37,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might still be attending to the sense objects, but not at the expense of ignoring the necessary basis for the sense objects.

102
00:10:37,849 --> 00:10:38,670
[SPEAKER_00]: You can go even further.

103
00:10:39,883 --> 00:10:46,813
[SPEAKER_00]: You can discern the yoniso, manasikāra, of your current... currently... currently performed action.

104
00:10:46,873 --> 00:10:48,455
[SPEAKER_00]: So, say you are engaged in some action.

105
00:10:48,495 --> 00:10:49,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Say you're writing something.

106
00:10:50,458 --> 00:10:52,540
[SPEAKER_00]: You're writing a letter, as an example.

107
00:10:53,522 --> 00:10:55,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention to write a letter.

108
00:10:56,961 --> 00:11:11,352
[SPEAKER_00]: That binds all these acts of getting the paper, taking the pen, opening the paper, writing down the sentences, ideas, what you want to say, that whole thing, it's all contained within still having the intention to finish, to write and complete this letter.

109
00:11:12,332 --> 00:11:20,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if halfway through that letter, intention to not write a letter, the state of mind changes, you stop writing the letter.

110
00:11:20,779 --> 00:11:24,942
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another thing that the people would overlook, ignore or be unaware of,

111
00:11:25,874 --> 00:11:35,385
[SPEAKER_00]: When they act, they don't see their intentions that are behind those actions, giving the meaning to all the subsequent particular things you do to complete that action.

112
00:11:35,665 --> 00:11:38,088
[SPEAKER_00]: They remain unified with your original intention.

113
00:11:38,569 --> 00:11:39,870
[SPEAKER_00]: That intention doesn't go away.

114
00:11:40,411 --> 00:11:41,973
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not sitting there in the past.

115
00:11:42,353 --> 00:11:45,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's carried through by all these actions that you are engaging with.

116
00:11:46,678 --> 00:11:48,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these are the examples of discerning through the source.

117
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:53,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that, in and of itself, will not give rise to the right view.

118
00:11:53,524 --> 00:12:01,811
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're just discerning through the source, obviously, it would give rise to some degree of wisdom and some degree of withdrawal from being overly absorbed in sense objects.

119
00:12:02,511 --> 00:12:05,754
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it won't give rise to the right view, even in and of itself.

120
00:12:05,854 --> 00:12:08,817
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if somebody does have that way of attending, not overlooking,

121
00:12:09,817 --> 00:12:17,019
[SPEAKER_00]: A degree that's hiding there in the middle, like your eyes, are hiding between, oh, me here, and looking at things over there, that you just assume.

122
00:12:18,499 --> 00:12:28,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, if you have developed that recognition, and you get the right instruction, that points at that middle way, the utterance of another, the right view is inevitable.

123
00:12:29,622 --> 00:12:31,043
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot not occur.

124
00:12:31,523 --> 00:12:32,123
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

125
00:12:32,843 --> 00:12:35,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's exactly what the Buddha describes it here.

126
00:12:35,244 --> 00:12:35,504
[SPEAKER_00]: He's like,

127
00:12:36,510 --> 00:12:43,924
[SPEAKER_00]: The ending of the defilement, so basically the liberation from suffering, is for the one who knows and sees, not for the one who doesn't know and see.

128
00:12:44,725 --> 00:12:45,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Know and see what?

129
00:12:45,647 --> 00:12:48,372
[SPEAKER_00]: That middle ground, that which is usually invisible,

130
00:12:49,387 --> 00:12:59,773
[SPEAKER_00]: In people's actions, in people's perception of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, stuff that they overlook, stuff that they need to be pointed at in order to start to discern it.

131
00:13:00,133 --> 00:13:02,094
[SPEAKER_00]: The necessary basis of certain things.

132
00:13:02,654 --> 00:13:04,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Even, again, you will never be able to see your eyes.

133
00:13:05,256 --> 00:13:06,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you can hammer in

134
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:18,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, on the individual basis of every perception, and in general, that it's impossible, it will be impossible to see if eye organs stop working.

135
00:13:18,049 --> 00:13:19,130
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inconceivable.

136
00:13:19,631 --> 00:13:31,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, simply, that which starts as an abstract thought of, oh, my eye organs are because of which I'm seeing this, start including into every time you're engaged with sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, respective organs.

137
00:13:32,805 --> 00:13:44,288
[SPEAKER_00]: You would not be able to experience any kind of sense of touch, presence of the body here and now, if there is not that living physical body over there, simultaneously existing.

138
00:13:44,328 --> 00:13:45,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Or over here, you can say.

139
00:13:46,048 --> 00:13:51,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is not to try to attend to what that source might be,

140
00:13:52,427 --> 00:13:58,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that would be on the level of already attending to the objects of your senses, including mentally.

141
00:13:59,113 --> 00:14:04,778
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to discern the necessity of the source while your attention is resting on this or that.

142
00:14:05,138 --> 00:14:16,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's how... that's why sense restraint is necessary, to just force you to withdraw from the dependence on the sense objects and the degree that your mind kind of is engaged with.

143
00:14:16,988 --> 00:14:23,472
[SPEAKER_00]: And once you're withdrawn from that overly proliferated dependence on the sense objects, you actually then get to... well, you could.

144
00:14:23,792 --> 00:14:34,199
[SPEAKER_00]: There is at least some possibility that then you get to discern, ah, necessity of the phenomenon of the living eyes that exists simultaneous to the things I'm seeing and I'm engaged with.

145
00:14:35,019 --> 00:14:40,243
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just to that degree, you don't need to then seek and find out what those eyes really are.

146
00:14:40,952 --> 00:14:41,352
[SPEAKER_00]: You want.

147
00:14:41,732 --> 00:14:51,497
[SPEAKER_00]: What those eyes really are, is that an invisible aspect that's simultaneously present in the opposite direction of whatever you are currently attending.

148
00:14:51,557 --> 00:14:52,958
[SPEAKER_00]: In the nutshell, that's yoniso, manasikāra.

149
00:14:52,978 --> 00:15:02,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see, okay, you could still take it to mean wise reflection, but what if you take it to mean something as deep as I just described?

150
00:15:03,363 --> 00:15:05,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Something as hard to see as I just described?

151
00:15:06,825 --> 00:15:08,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it really far-fetched then?

152
00:15:08,286 --> 00:15:09,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, taking it to mean

153
00:15:11,899 --> 00:15:18,122
[SPEAKER_00]: In such manner, plus instruction of another, oh, yeah, then the right view is certainly more possible.

154
00:15:18,922 --> 00:15:20,483
[SPEAKER_00]: It certainly makes even more sense.

155
00:15:21,183 --> 00:15:38,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you are already discerning, already undermining everything you see, hear, smell, taste, touch, think for that matter, everything you do, you're undermining by recognition of the necessary basis for it, that cannot be yours at the same time, that's not yours, that has its own field, that has its own existence.

156
00:15:39,587 --> 00:15:57,274
[SPEAKER_00]: That has its own course, that has its own characteristics, then you recognize, oh, okay, if I start seeing my whole experience undermined in that manner, and I get the right instruction of where the dukkha is, it's it's it's very likely I will be able to understand it.

157
00:15:58,175 --> 00:15:58,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But,

158
00:15:59,913 --> 00:16:12,226
[SPEAKER_00]: If I remain just regarding reading the sutta and thinking yoniso is just that wise reflection, just sit down and consider things before you do them, which is nice, nobody denies that, but it's not deep enough.

159
00:16:12,266 --> 00:16:17,712
[SPEAKER_00]: It does not undermine your sense of presence here and now enough for the dhamma to apply.

160
00:16:18,818 --> 00:16:20,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's kind of the point.

161
00:16:20,098 --> 00:16:24,980
[SPEAKER_00]: And often, we'll come across that, especially if we go through these sutta studies.

162
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:28,481
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like that common translations are wrong.

163
00:16:28,541 --> 00:16:30,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just they're not accurate enough.

164
00:16:31,662 --> 00:16:38,405
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see, like, say, if you are rightly considering the necessary basis, the necessity of

165
00:16:39,257 --> 00:16:53,122
[SPEAKER_00]: The organs, living, functioning organs of your eyes, simultaneously present while you're looking at this thing now, any lust, any aversion, whatever might be, that level of engagement, will be bracketed, will be put second, to the necessary basis for it.

166
00:16:53,162 --> 00:17:02,085
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's like, eye organ, sights, or anything in between, as the suttas often describe, he experienced this passion towards, because it's undermined rightly.

167
00:17:02,745 --> 00:17:08,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when and whenever there is absence, whenever there is lust, aversion, delusion undermined, there is a rising of wisdom.

168
00:17:08,808 --> 00:17:17,754
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could see that if you take yoniso to mean this, what we are just... what I'm describing here and now and now, it's like, oh yeah, that includes wise reflection, for sure.

169
00:17:18,214 --> 00:17:26,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you cannot unwisely... reflect unwisely while you're simultaneously undermining your lust, aversion, loss of perspective with the right order.

170
00:17:27,728 --> 00:17:39,657
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is no lust, or there is diminishing of lust, diminishing of aversion, diminishing of delusion, there is pretty much... non... there is an increase in the right perspective.

171
00:17:40,818 --> 00:17:46,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not lustful, you're less likely... well, if you're not lustful, you will not be unreasonable.

172
00:17:47,003 --> 00:17:53,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever people are unreasonable, whenever they lose context, lose perspective, it's on account of lust, aversion or delusion.

173
00:17:53,708 --> 00:17:54,769
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no other option there.

174
00:17:56,330 --> 00:18:08,317
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you take yoniso to mean this, and then work towards understanding it, then you will need very little instruction to apply, because this is already 50% of the work.

175
00:18:09,177 --> 00:18:12,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why it's so important, and the whole sutta starts with that.

176
00:18:13,806 --> 00:18:18,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Ending of suffering, ending of defilements, is for the one who sees, not for the one who doesn't see.

177
00:18:18,388 --> 00:18:18,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't see what?

178
00:18:19,649 --> 00:18:22,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Yoniso manasikāra and ayoniso manasikāra.

179
00:18:22,210 --> 00:18:26,792
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees the degree, he sees the extent, he sees that simultaneous relationship.

180
00:18:28,733 --> 00:18:30,254
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, continues.

181
00:18:31,074 --> 00:18:36,537
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you attend not through the source, defilements, greed, aversion, delusion, increase.

182
00:18:38,317 --> 00:18:39,538
[SPEAKER_00]: And once arisen, they grow.

183
00:18:40,942 --> 00:18:45,206
[SPEAKER_00]: When you attend through the source, defilements don't arise, and once arisen, they diminish.

184
00:18:46,307 --> 00:18:57,016
[SPEAKER_00]: And that... that's actually in another sutta, where the Buddha talks about hindrances, where people commonly think hindrances... you deal with hindrances by countering them, by doing this when this hindrance is present, by doing that when this hindrance is present.

185
00:18:57,497 --> 00:19:00,940
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, the whole sutta sums up the whole five hindrances.

186
00:19:01,420 --> 00:19:04,503
[SPEAKER_00]: You deal with them by seeing through them with yoniso manasikāra.

187
00:19:05,630 --> 00:19:06,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

188
00:19:06,591 --> 00:19:12,914
[SPEAKER_00]: See, yoniso manasikāra is a factor that Putujana can develop with the right instruction from another.

189
00:19:13,695 --> 00:19:17,197
[SPEAKER_00]: He can become a Sotāpanna and that yoniso manasikāra doesn't... it's not finished.

190
00:19:17,998 --> 00:19:29,925
[SPEAKER_00]: The entire, like, development of that practice that follows from a Sotāpanna to an Arhant is just done through expanding the yoniso manasikāra until there is no part of your experience

191
00:19:31,442 --> 00:19:36,766
[SPEAKER_00]: No part of your aggregates that you could possibly attend wrongly with ayoniso.

192
00:19:37,487 --> 00:19:45,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that habit of seeing with yoniso is thoroughly, has thoroughly permeated all the aspects of your experience.

193
00:19:45,993 --> 00:19:51,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Even like the five hindrances, abandoning them, you abandon them by seeing through them.

194
00:19:52,659 --> 00:19:57,642
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by seeing their source, the necessary present basis of an arisen sensuality,

195
00:19:58,688 --> 00:20:08,231
[SPEAKER_00]: You see it as a necessary present basis of what is in the subha image, a picture of beautiful, image of beautiful, notion of beautiful, that you haven't undermined.

196
00:20:08,911 --> 00:20:14,432
[SPEAKER_00]: You undermine it there and then, that which was the hindrance remains, you become unhindered by it.

197
00:20:15,093 --> 00:20:24,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And it goes on for all the other... We'll come to that sutta at some point, and obviously I'll reference it here, but the point is that...

198
00:20:24,335 --> 00:20:27,316
[SPEAKER_00]: In the United Sutta, yes, actually the Buddha said there is not a single thing

199
00:20:28,115 --> 00:20:35,403
[SPEAKER_00]: That makes centrality... makes the arisen centrality increase, non-arisen centrality arise, such as ayoniso.

200
00:20:36,625 --> 00:20:41,050
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is not a single thing in the world that makes the arisen centrality diminish,

201
00:20:42,043 --> 00:20:46,787
[SPEAKER_00]: And non-arisen sensuality not arise as yoniso manasikāra.

202
00:20:47,127 --> 00:21:02,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, learning how to undermine things rightly, through attending with the source, in other words, whatever you're attending, you're discerning the source present there simultaneously with it, is the way they would free you, not just from sensuality, but from the hindrances and from all the defilements.

203
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:04,181
[SPEAKER_00]: And this sutta just confirms that.

204
00:21:04,701 --> 00:21:09,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when the Buddha describes these types of defilements, and then we go now to the first defilement, which is

205
00:21:10,317 --> 00:21:12,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Defilement to be given up by seeing.

206
00:21:13,518 --> 00:21:16,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, what are the defilements that should be given up by seeing?

207
00:21:17,679 --> 00:21:24,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Take an untrained ordinary person, putujana, who is neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the noble ones, hasn't seen them, has not discerned them.

208
00:21:25,041 --> 00:21:26,201
[SPEAKER_00]: They've not seen the true person.

209
00:21:26,221 --> 00:21:29,462
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they don't have the right instruction, they don't have yoniso manasikāra.

210
00:21:30,222 --> 00:21:31,922
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't... they don't understand.

211
00:21:32,402 --> 00:21:35,583
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see for themselves, internally, intimately.

212
00:21:35,783 --> 00:21:38,344
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see which things they should attend,

213
00:21:38,992 --> 00:21:45,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, which things they should allow their attention to rest on, and which things they should not allow their attention to rest on.

214
00:21:46,178 --> 00:21:53,865
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to see which things you should allow your attention to rest on, and which things that aren't suitable for attention, you need to become a sotāpanna first.

215
00:21:54,886 --> 00:21:56,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, putujana cannot discern that.

216
00:21:57,128 --> 00:22:00,771
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they attend things they shouldn't, and they don't attend things they should.

217
00:22:00,851 --> 00:22:03,193
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't know where the emphasis is, as I often say.

218
00:22:04,930 --> 00:22:08,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there... and then explains what are those things that they attend and they shouldn't.

219
00:22:08,893 --> 00:22:15,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there are things that, when attended to, give rise to unoriginal defilements and make the original defilements grow.

220
00:22:15,838 --> 00:22:21,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Defilements of sensual desire, defilements of being, bhāva and ignorance.

221
00:22:21,202 --> 00:22:23,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there are things that they attend but they shouldn't.

222
00:22:23,704 --> 00:22:28,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... they're attending them and the way they're attending them, that's another mistake.

223
00:22:29,480 --> 00:22:38,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, there are things that if you stop attending in that wrong way, they will not give rise to unarisen, unwholesome things and defilements.

224
00:22:39,885 --> 00:22:48,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't attend sensual desire, if you don't attend being wrongly, if you don't attend ignorance, those things will not increase.

225
00:22:49,870 --> 00:22:56,253
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in order to know that, in order to discern those things, you already need to have the right view and you already need to have yoniso manasikāra, obviously.

226
00:22:59,409 --> 00:23:13,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having... not knowing... Putujana not knowing what should he attend, he attends to things, he attends to unsuitable things, he attends to suitable things wrongly, he... basically, he doesn't know where the measure is.

227
00:23:14,938 --> 00:23:20,881
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when they're not attending through the source, ayoniso, manasikaroti,

228
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:25,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the questions such as, did I exist in the past?

229
00:23:26,505 --> 00:23:27,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Did I not exist in the past?

230
00:23:28,348 --> 00:23:29,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Was I in the past?

231
00:23:29,769 --> 00:23:30,831
[SPEAKER_00]: How was I in the past?

232
00:23:31,432 --> 00:23:33,134
[SPEAKER_00]: After being what, what did I become?

233
00:23:34,975 --> 00:23:36,296
[SPEAKER_00]: The questions about the future.

234
00:23:36,336 --> 00:23:37,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I exist in the future?

235
00:23:37,517 --> 00:23:38,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I not exist in the future?

236
00:23:38,738 --> 00:23:39,599
[SPEAKER_00]: What will be the future?

237
00:23:39,639 --> 00:23:40,599
[SPEAKER_00]: How will I be in the future?

238
00:23:40,619 --> 00:23:42,161
[SPEAKER_00]: After being what, what will I become?

239
00:23:43,101 --> 00:23:44,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Or they are undecided about the present.

240
00:23:45,223 --> 00:23:45,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I?

241
00:23:45,703 --> 00:23:46,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I not?

242
00:23:46,304 --> 00:23:46,804
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I?

243
00:23:46,864 --> 00:23:47,325
[SPEAKER_00]: How am I?

244
00:23:47,505 --> 00:23:48,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Is everything anicca?

245
00:23:48,846 --> 00:23:49,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is anicca.

246
00:23:49,566 --> 00:23:50,467
[SPEAKER_00]: There is really anatta.

247
00:23:50,507 --> 00:23:51,268
[SPEAKER_00]: This is anatta.

248
00:23:51,628 --> 00:23:52,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is anatta.

249
00:23:52,489 --> 00:23:53,269
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said...

250
00:23:53,349 --> 00:23:55,471
[SPEAKER_00]: But fundamentally, they don't see that for themselves.

251
00:23:55,491 --> 00:23:59,994
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's all just on the level of concerned guesswork, so to speak.

252
00:24:00,975 --> 00:24:09,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all a result of cultivating ayoniso manasikāra, of not seeing things through the source that they have arisen.

253
00:24:10,199 --> 00:24:21,824
[SPEAKER_00]: And by doing so, yes, by doing the ayoniso manasikāra, you, of course, inevitably, inadvertently, put yourself as the center of the experience.

254
00:24:21,884 --> 00:24:22,884
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what's emphasized.

255
00:24:23,064 --> 00:24:25,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Me seeing, me hearing, and so on.

256
00:24:25,685 --> 00:24:29,227
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much that route of ownership and route of all the problems.

257
00:24:29,267 --> 00:24:29,607
[SPEAKER_00]: So then,

258
00:24:30,278 --> 00:24:35,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Through continuing such ways of thinking and emphasizing things wrongly,

259
00:24:36,431 --> 00:24:41,693
[SPEAKER_00]: The natural questions, the natural concerns of the patujana would be, where did I come from?

260
00:24:42,214 --> 00:24:42,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Where will I go?

261
00:24:43,014 --> 00:24:47,956
[SPEAKER_00]: All these insecurities that revolve around me and my placement and my security.

262
00:24:48,456 --> 00:24:54,459
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if they don't think about the past or don't think about the future, presently they will be unclear and undecided.

263
00:24:54,799 --> 00:25:00,002
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they will still hold onto that view of themselves as the center of the experience.

264
00:25:00,282 --> 00:25:03,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they haven't undermined the center of the experience sufficiently enough.

265
00:25:04,643 --> 00:25:05,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what Buddha said.

266
00:25:05,643 --> 00:25:15,647
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stop attending wrongly to lust, to aversion, to being, to delusion, to ownership, to all these things, this will diminish.

267
00:25:15,907 --> 00:25:34,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're still putujana, so even if you still hold the self-view to some degree, if you haven't heard the Buddha's teaching, if you don't know the way out of the dukkha, by not attending wrongly to all these things that should not be attended to wrongly, these views will diminish and you will not have the pressure in question of

268
00:25:35,570 --> 00:25:36,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Where am I heading?

269
00:25:36,492 --> 00:25:37,254
[SPEAKER_00]: What will I be?

270
00:25:37,314 --> 00:25:38,597
[SPEAKER_00]: What's my future, you know?

271
00:25:38,977 --> 00:25:39,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be liberated?

272
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:43,888
[SPEAKER_00]: All these these anxious kind of things that concern oneself deeply.

273
00:25:46,435 --> 00:26:10,357
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then it says, so if they continue, when they don't, so when they're attending to things wrongly, do not discern yoniso and keep attending to things with ayoniso manasikāra, without the source, so to speak, without the recognition of the source, and then they continue engaging with such questions, was I in the past, what was, how did this come to be, and oh, what's saṃsara, and how did you originate, where we are heading, all that kind of

274
00:26:11,058 --> 00:26:18,497
[SPEAKER_00]: These answers that people seek to basically provide a sense of clarity and security.

275
00:26:19,284 --> 00:26:21,586
[SPEAKER_00]: For their concern about their future selves.

276
00:26:21,646 --> 00:26:22,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Where will I end up?

277
00:26:22,486 --> 00:26:23,367
[SPEAKER_00]: What will happen to me?

278
00:26:23,387 --> 00:26:24,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Will it go up or down?

279
00:26:24,468 --> 00:26:28,931
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, all these anxious things that will kind of arise at some point.

280
00:26:29,832 --> 00:26:45,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person continues doing that and tries seeking answer on that level, already on the basis of all the wrong assumptions and ayoniso, the Buddha says, when they attend not through the source in this way, one of the following six views arises for them.

281
00:26:45,303 --> 00:26:46,124
[SPEAKER_00]: There is myself,

282
00:26:46,885 --> 00:26:51,826
[SPEAKER_00]: What view cannot arise in them is, I perceive not-self with not-self.

283
00:26:51,887 --> 00:26:59,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for that, they need to have had undermined their experience sufficiently enough through yoniso manasikāra.

284
00:27:10,550 --> 00:27:19,519
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why often you have people talk about, oh, yes, you know, the the modern science has proven, the Buddha as well, that everything is impermanent, thus nothing is real.

285
00:27:19,559 --> 00:27:21,161
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's very much the wrong view.

286
00:27:22,342 --> 00:27:24,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not a question that things are not real.

287
00:27:24,925 --> 00:27:29,530
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is that things are not yours, even if you want them to be.

288
00:27:30,412 --> 00:27:34,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this sense of self, this center of experience that you are, you can deny.

289
00:27:34,815 --> 00:27:40,679
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say, well, this sense of self is just composed out of atoms and chemicals in this body and thus it's not mine.

290
00:27:41,099 --> 00:27:45,102
[SPEAKER_00]: But you still see that not-self that you described through your sense of self.

291
00:27:46,583 --> 00:27:48,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is ayoniso.

292
00:27:49,133 --> 00:27:50,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Or other way around.

293
00:27:50,675 --> 00:27:52,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Through the sense of not-self, you see self.

294
00:27:52,998 --> 00:28:03,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Either permutation, whichever way you reflect back, there's going to be a sense of self hiding there, or implicit there, because you can't just willingly step outside of it.

295
00:28:03,110 --> 00:28:06,194
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only undermine it through yoniso and the right instruction.

296
00:28:06,855 --> 00:28:13,641
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not incidental that these six views, there is self, or there is no self, which you say, which you perceive, which you send to self.

297
00:28:14,322 --> 00:28:15,663
[SPEAKER_00]: I perceive the self with self.

298
00:28:15,963 --> 00:28:17,124
[SPEAKER_00]: There is self on every level.

299
00:28:17,244 --> 00:28:17,564
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

300
00:28:17,765 --> 00:28:18,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Some things are not self.

301
00:28:18,685 --> 00:28:19,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Some things are self.

302
00:28:19,846 --> 00:28:21,848
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not myself.

303
00:28:22,208 --> 00:28:23,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Yesterday was myself.

304
00:28:23,430 --> 00:28:24,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Today is not myself.

305
00:28:24,591 --> 00:28:30,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, past, future, present, impersonal, impersonal, there's going to be self hiding at some level.

306
00:28:30,295 --> 00:28:32,998
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, wrong emphasis would be hiding at some level.

307
00:28:34,365 --> 00:28:47,290
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not incidental that within this six views, so these are the five, and then the sixth view is the self of mind is he, the speaker, the knower, who experiences the result of good-bad.

308
00:28:47,630 --> 00:28:50,772
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, this is like, obviously, a template phrase.

309
00:28:51,432 --> 00:28:53,893
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could read the subtlety into and recognize it.

310
00:28:54,173 --> 00:28:59,655
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, when people say, oh, yeah, nothing's real, everything is anatta, there is just the knowing of the experience.

311
00:28:59,675 --> 00:29:06,898
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just the knower, the pure consciousness, the eternal chitta, all these things that people say, all those things are personified to that extent.

312
00:29:07,478 --> 00:29:12,040
[SPEAKER_00]: All of those things is now what the self has become and where it is hiding.

313
00:29:12,060 --> 00:29:14,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how you rebrand it or how you call it.

314
00:29:14,781 --> 00:29:18,443
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are giving an entity, a phenomenon, superior position,

315
00:29:19,239 --> 00:29:25,726
[SPEAKER_00]: From which the knowing happens, and perception, and liberation, and eternal freedom, that entity is going to be your sense of self.

316
00:29:26,647 --> 00:29:28,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what you start with.

317
00:29:28,389 --> 00:29:34,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is not finding the super special thing that can be your sense of self, that's anatta, and that's free from everything.

318
00:29:35,136 --> 00:29:39,100
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is undermining everything until you can't hold on to things even if you want to.

319
00:29:40,130 --> 00:29:52,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why nobody can do the practice of the right view accidentally, because it goes against the entire being that you are, against the grain of it, against everything that your inclination would go.

320
00:29:52,121 --> 00:29:56,105
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to swim against them, swim against the stream, as the Buddha described.

321
00:29:56,545 --> 00:29:59,628
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, you can't do that accidentally.

322
00:29:59,768 --> 00:30:04,012
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to have had some sort of faith, in some sort of instruction, that is now making you

323
00:30:04,673 --> 00:30:14,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Constantly undermine your experience through seeing the necessary present source, here and now, and by doing so, to that extent, make yourself unable to own

324
00:30:15,318 --> 00:30:20,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Or assume the ownership or appropriate things that you're perceiving, like your senses.

325
00:30:20,142 --> 00:30:33,713
[SPEAKER_00]: See, by being... by overlooking your eyes and being engaged with the sight, delighting in it, relishing it, you have appropriated your eyes, simply by not seeing that as a necessary basis over there, independent of of your seeing, so to speak.

326
00:30:33,993 --> 00:30:36,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Independent in a sense of it's not in your control.

327
00:30:37,616 --> 00:30:41,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not incidental that these views do not allow

328
00:30:42,149 --> 00:30:48,112
[SPEAKER_00]: For somebody to perceive not-self with not-self, top to bottom, to be free from the ātavāda, from the self-view.

329
00:30:48,792 --> 00:30:53,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for that, they need to have had stopped attending to these things with ayoniso.

330
00:30:53,615 --> 00:31:04,280
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order to do that, they need to have had been sufficiently restrained, withdrawn, so they're not giving the the attention to things they shouldn't be giving.

331
00:31:04,300 --> 00:31:04,780
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

332
00:31:06,567 --> 00:31:16,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha concludes this by saying, this is called going into views, a thicket of views, a desert of views, a contortion of views, a fetter of views, a writhing of views.

333
00:31:19,355 --> 00:31:26,939
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, being fettered by these views, an untrained ordinary person is not freed from birth, old age, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness and distress.

334
00:31:27,519 --> 00:31:29,220
[SPEAKER_00]: They are not free from suffering, I say.

335
00:31:30,281 --> 00:31:33,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, take the trained noble disciple, the one with the right view,

336
00:31:34,242 --> 00:31:40,244
[SPEAKER_00]: They understand for themselves which things they should attend and which things they should not attend.

337
00:31:41,044 --> 00:31:50,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And they understand that things that they shouldn't attend is the one that make the defilements arise and non-arisen manifest and the arisen one grow, such as sensuality, being and ignorance.

338
00:31:50,886 --> 00:31:51,707
[SPEAKER_00]: So it repeats this.

339
00:31:51,827 --> 00:31:57,068
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they understand that the things that they... if they are attended to, they don't give rise to these unwholesome states.

340
00:31:58,680 --> 00:32:00,101
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then they stick to it.

341
00:32:00,262 --> 00:32:04,005
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't attend to things they shouldn't and attend to the things they should.

342
00:32:04,746 --> 00:32:09,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the un-arisen defilements don't arise and those defilements that are arisen, they're given up.

343
00:32:11,112 --> 00:32:12,393
[SPEAKER_00]: They attend through the source.

344
00:32:12,754 --> 00:32:18,479
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they recognize the nature of their situation here and now.

345
00:32:18,679 --> 00:32:21,863
[SPEAKER_00]: See, when you attend through the source of the

346
00:32:22,758 --> 00:32:26,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Necessity of the eye organs for your sights.

347
00:32:27,020 --> 00:32:28,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, just take overall.

348
00:32:28,200 --> 00:32:34,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Necessity of this physical lump of flesh, which is the body, for your experience as a whole.

349
00:32:34,022 --> 00:32:39,824
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a necessary basis while you're attending particular aspect of the... aspects of the experience as a whole.

350
00:32:40,285 --> 00:32:49,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, when you're attending to that, with the right instruction, you get to recognize the certain characteristic of that necessary basis, of that condition.

351
00:32:50,695 --> 00:33:07,408
[SPEAKER_00]: of those eye organs, or the body as a whole, which is... it's impermanent, it's not pleasurable, being paired with a moving vehicle that you have no control over its directions, it's actually, when you recognize, oh, this is uneasy,

352
00:33:08,972 --> 00:33:09,993
[SPEAKER_00]: This, this cannot be.

353
00:33:10,333 --> 00:33:17,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Any pleasure I experience is contingent to the fact that this vehicle is still moving in the direction that can lead to my tremendous peril.

354
00:33:18,477 --> 00:33:25,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, simple recognition of un-ownability of the body on that yoniso level.

355
00:33:25,340 --> 00:33:28,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, oh, this is not mine, this is not mine, this is unownable.

356
00:33:28,142 --> 00:33:28,382
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

357
00:33:28,902 --> 00:33:36,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing it as a necessary basis for attending to it, even if it's the same body you're attending, that necessary basis is unownable.

358
00:33:37,160 --> 00:33:38,721
[SPEAKER_00]: That source is unownable.

359
00:33:39,261 --> 00:33:42,623
[SPEAKER_00]: That source, thus, because it's unownable, came on its own.

360
00:33:42,663 --> 00:33:44,444
[SPEAKER_00]: It's subject to its own directions.

361
00:33:44,924 --> 00:33:45,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

362
00:33:45,985 --> 00:33:46,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't see you.

363
00:33:46,705 --> 00:33:47,826
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for it.

364
00:33:48,386 --> 00:33:50,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it cannot be in your possession eternally.

365
00:33:50,927 --> 00:33:52,628
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot remain unchanged internally.

366
00:33:52,908 --> 00:33:54,389
[SPEAKER_00]: And that all becomes implicit.

367
00:33:54,910 --> 00:34:01,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why Venusoddhapanna, who has stopped attending to wrong things, who has developed yoniso manasikāra,

368
00:34:02,432 --> 00:34:06,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Who has received the instruction of what to look for in terms of suffering, he recognizes.

369
00:34:06,233 --> 00:34:09,635
[SPEAKER_00]: When he attends through the source, he recognizes this is the suffering.

370
00:34:10,795 --> 00:34:12,136
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the origin of suffering.

371
00:34:12,856 --> 00:34:14,457
[SPEAKER_00]: This will be the ending of that suffering.

372
00:34:14,877 --> 00:34:17,978
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the practice that would cement that cessation of that suffering.

373
00:34:18,158 --> 00:34:19,238
[SPEAKER_00]: That would cement that freedom.

374
00:34:19,959 --> 00:34:21,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's exactly what the suffering is.

375
00:34:21,399 --> 00:34:26,081
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in this or this, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, past, future.

376
00:34:26,121 --> 00:34:26,461
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

377
00:34:26,601 --> 00:34:28,942
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the perpetual relationship

378
00:34:29,573 --> 00:34:43,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Between the necessary basis and your life, and things you're engaged with, with the necessary basis of that source and things you're attending, through that... well, with or without that recognition of that source, that's where the suffering is.

379
00:34:43,222 --> 00:34:49,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Once he recognizes that, all the concerns about past, future, who he was, what he will be, where he's heading, it's all diminished.

380
00:34:50,667 --> 00:34:56,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when somebody recognizes the relationship between the source and things they're attending at the same time,

381
00:34:56,787 --> 00:34:59,891
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the source of their experience of attending to this thing or that thing.

382
00:35:00,672 --> 00:35:02,574
[SPEAKER_00]: They have recognized the dhamma.

383
00:35:03,575 --> 00:35:07,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And by doing so, they give up the three fetters, as the sutta describes.

384
00:35:07,601 --> 00:35:13,348
[SPEAKER_00]: They give the ownership view, sakāyā deity or personality view, depending on what translation you look at.

385
00:35:14,377 --> 00:35:20,400
[SPEAKER_00]: They give doubt and they give grasping of the virtues and virtue practices or holding on to.

386
00:35:20,440 --> 00:35:21,401
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still about the paramāsa.

387
00:35:21,581 --> 00:35:22,862
[SPEAKER_00]: We have other talks on that as well.

388
00:35:23,002 --> 00:35:25,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe we'll link that too.

389
00:35:25,543 --> 00:35:37,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, they give a wrong view of there is a self that's the center of the experience, independent of these aggregates, independent of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, that perceives, smells, tastes, sees, and so on, hears.

390
00:35:37,969 --> 00:35:38,810
[SPEAKER_00]: They give up doubt.

391
00:35:39,810 --> 00:35:48,277
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to what the origin is, what the actual source is, they can still doubt mundane stuff, but they can't doubt the presence of the source while they're doubting.

392
00:35:49,037 --> 00:35:51,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why it becomes inaccessible to doubt.

393
00:35:51,740 --> 00:35:54,422
[SPEAKER_00]: See, when you're in the experience of doubting, you're attending to your doubt.

394
00:35:55,002 --> 00:36:00,106
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the present source enduring as a necessary basis for you to be engaged in the experience of doubt.

395
00:36:00,266 --> 00:36:01,147
[SPEAKER_00]: It is real as such.

396
00:36:01,808 --> 00:36:06,992
[SPEAKER_00]: And they have thoroughly discerned that, and that's inaccessible to doubt, as the suttas would describe that.

397
00:36:09,551 --> 00:36:19,678
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they give up doubt and then they give up the dependence, the sense, the view of duty that the practice is on the level of external mechanical things that come through your sense objects.

398
00:36:20,378 --> 00:36:24,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, by doing this, by doing that, by holding on to this, by believing that.

399
00:36:24,841 --> 00:36:35,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, techniques, methods, external authorities, all that is given up because that's never where the practice was and that's never where the suffering was.

400
00:36:38,130 --> 00:36:40,032
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, number two...

401
00:36:43,315 --> 00:36:46,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Number two are the defilements to be given up by restraint.

402
00:36:48,520 --> 00:36:53,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And the word for restraint is samvara, kept in check, basically, restrained.

403
00:36:53,845 --> 00:37:00,572
[SPEAKER_00]: So, take a monk who, reflecting through the source, so, patisankha yoniso,

404
00:37:01,786 --> 00:37:03,788
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives restraining the faculty of the eye.

405
00:37:03,868 --> 00:37:08,672
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, reflecting through the source, it's like broadening of your yoniso manasikāra.

406
00:37:08,692 --> 00:37:10,613
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like increasing the basis.

407
00:37:10,853 --> 00:37:11,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a practice.

408
00:37:12,014 --> 00:37:22,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have yoniso, you see the necessary source, and now you're increasing that basis of that source, so that you don't actively have to keep reminding yourself, but it stays there as a context.

409
00:37:23,624 --> 00:37:28,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being established in such habit of reflecting

410
00:37:29,455 --> 00:37:34,500
[SPEAKER_00]: through yoniso manasikāra, or with yoniso manasikāra, he lives restraining the faculty of the eye.

411
00:37:35,381 --> 00:37:41,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he restrains his eyes from seeing things that would give rise to lust and so on, even before he sees them.

412
00:37:42,247 --> 00:37:44,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he recognizes that, eh...

413
00:37:46,623 --> 00:37:54,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Distressing and feverish defilement that might arise in someone who lives without the restraint, will not arise in me if I live with restraint.

414
00:37:54,985 --> 00:37:57,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's already undermined unwholesome.

415
00:37:57,886 --> 00:37:58,967
[SPEAKER_00]: He recognizes unwholesome.

416
00:37:58,987 --> 00:38:00,727
[SPEAKER_00]: He's undermined on the level of the view.

417
00:38:01,387 --> 00:38:14,291
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, he needs to practically practice, engage with, preventing his eyes from going back to these... the remnants of these old habits of seeking pleasure in sights and forgetting about the necessary basis of the living body, the source that's unownable, that's

418
00:38:14,611 --> 00:38:16,912
[SPEAKER_00]: That's danger, that's peril, that's like a chopping block.

419
00:38:17,492 --> 00:38:23,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Not forgetting that, is then maintained, simply through not allowing your eyes to distract themselves.

420
00:38:23,215 --> 00:38:25,516
[SPEAKER_00]: That's in the natural, the purpose of the restraint.

421
00:38:26,016 --> 00:38:36,841
[SPEAKER_00]: You start doing it externally, so that then, becomes this internal criteria of yoniso manasikāra is developed, and then you maintain that, simply through not allowing your eyes to look at things that they shouldn't.

422
00:38:37,950 --> 00:38:52,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it becomes like an effortless maintenance and development of yoniso manasikāra, which means it becomes like develop... effortless development of the path, simply through not doing certain things, not letting your eyes look at unwholesome sights.

423
00:38:52,483 --> 00:38:59,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And same goes like reflecting through a source, restraining the faculty of ear, nose, tongue, body and the mind.

424
00:39:00,606 --> 00:39:03,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And these are the defilements that should be given up by restraint.

425
00:39:03,928 --> 00:39:17,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, somebody without, say, a puthujjana, without yoniso manasikāra, if he practices restraint, he will also not give rise to these states that will beset the mind with defilements and so on.

426
00:39:18,176 --> 00:39:27,081
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in and of itself, that restraint will not bring yoniso manasikāra to increase and become fortified, because they haven't developed that.

427
00:39:27,801 --> 00:39:34,144
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should still practice sense restraint, even if you were not a sotāpanna, because that can help you develop yoniso manasikāra.

428
00:39:34,644 --> 00:39:39,066
[SPEAKER_00]: But this practice here, it's done through yoniso manasikāra, which is why, in return, it feeds it.

429
00:39:39,686 --> 00:39:46,048
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like, by simply not engaging with unwholesome sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, one's yoniso is developed.

430
00:39:46,148 --> 00:39:53,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a certain... a whole... a whole layer, for lack of a better word, of defilements are simply given up through restraint.

431
00:39:54,372 --> 00:39:54,752
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

432
00:39:55,514 --> 00:39:58,016
[SPEAKER_00]: For the one with the right view, not for the one without the right view.

433
00:39:58,136 --> 00:39:59,036
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll keep repeating that.

434
00:40:01,018 --> 00:40:02,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Number three.

435
00:40:02,039 --> 00:40:03,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Defilements given up by using.

436
00:40:04,780 --> 00:40:05,901
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are those defilements?

437
00:40:05,941 --> 00:40:10,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, take a monk who, reflecting through the source, makes use of robes.

438
00:40:11,365 --> 00:40:18,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Only for the sake of warding off cold, heat, touch of flies, mosquitoes, wind, sun, reptiles, for covering up the private parts.

439
00:40:18,910 --> 00:40:21,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting through the source, he makes the use of alms food.

440
00:40:22,072 --> 00:40:30,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for fun, not for adornment, not for decoration, but only to sustain the body, avoid the harm, support the spiritual practice, avoid illness and stuff like that.

441
00:40:32,256 --> 00:40:36,597
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this way, I shall put an end to all discomfort, not give rise to new discomfort, and I will live blamelessly and at ease.

442
00:40:37,177 --> 00:40:46,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting through the source, he makes the use of lodgings for the sake of warding off cold heat, touch of flies, mosquitoes, wind, sun, reptiles, from harsh weather, and to enjoy retreat, seclusion.

443
00:40:47,790 --> 00:40:56,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting through the source, he makes use of the medicines and supplies for the sick, only for the sake of warding off the pains of illness and promote good health.

444
00:40:56,560 --> 00:41:02,708
[SPEAKER_00]: For the distressing and feverish defilements might arise in someone who lives without using these things, they do not arise when they're used rightly.

445
00:41:03,807 --> 00:41:12,392
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this case, again, it's certain necessities, such as shelter, food, clothes, medicines.

446
00:41:13,352 --> 00:41:15,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Even those things are not taken for granted.

447
00:41:15,374 --> 00:41:17,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, I must do this because illness is bad.

448
00:41:17,715 --> 00:41:19,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, illness is bad.

449
00:41:19,296 --> 00:41:31,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Illness will prevent you, but any engagement with doing, with taking medicine, or having a shelter, or looking for a shelter, or looking for robes, is always maintained, kept in check.

450
00:41:32,238 --> 00:41:45,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Through maintaining, through reflecting, through the source, in this case, source being the wholesome context, the necessary context that's practically relevant for your practice.

451
00:41:46,335 --> 00:41:50,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he seeks the lodgings.

452
00:41:50,841 --> 00:42:01,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeking of the lodging is informed, is decided upon, if the criteria are met that are suitable for practice, not the criteria that come out of one's preference.

453
00:42:02,772 --> 00:42:03,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Or one's habits.

454
00:42:03,973 --> 00:42:05,974
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, what do I need for practice?

455
00:42:06,014 --> 00:42:12,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I need shelter from the elements, from the cold, I need not overly exposed and its occlusion.

456
00:42:13,238 --> 00:42:15,420
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you seek a place that meets that and that's it.

457
00:42:15,680 --> 00:42:19,282
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't go further, because the need has been met.

458
00:42:19,362 --> 00:42:21,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's what I was saying in the beginning.

459
00:42:21,043 --> 00:42:26,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, one of the ways of discerning yoniso manasikāra is through your engagement with things and why you're doing them.

460
00:42:27,387 --> 00:42:29,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are eating, why are you eating?

461
00:42:30,088 --> 00:42:35,449
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't ask that question yourself, you'll be eating for the wrong reasons, because that's how we all start.

462
00:42:36,070 --> 00:42:42,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to actively keep re-training your own intention.

463
00:42:42,451 --> 00:42:52,934
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, yes, you can ignore that, ignore the necessity of food for practice, and eat just because of pleasure, or because of boredom, or because of whatever else.

464
00:42:53,662 --> 00:42:54,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you can make the effort.

465
00:42:54,463 --> 00:42:54,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Hold on.

466
00:42:54,823 --> 00:42:55,223
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

467
00:42:55,824 --> 00:43:04,611
[SPEAKER_00]: I will recollect and frequently ponder and maintain the context that food is only so that I can practice ending of suffering.

468
00:43:05,332 --> 00:43:05,712
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

469
00:43:05,752 --> 00:43:06,192
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my...

470
00:43:06,633 --> 00:43:09,975
[SPEAKER_00]: I will allow myself only to that extent to engage with the food.

471
00:43:10,056 --> 00:43:20,004
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, see, when you're choosing what to eat, without losing that context, that yoniso, that source of your action of eating, it will be much less likely

472
00:43:21,065 --> 00:43:23,167
[SPEAKER_00]: that you will be choosing solely based on the preference.

473
00:43:23,847 --> 00:43:34,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be choosing more like what will last the longest, what will keep me the fullest for the smallest amount, what will not make me feel heavy and sluggish and sleepy, but what will provide the necessary nutrients.

474
00:43:36,682 --> 00:43:40,043
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that, and you have to maintain it every time you eat.

475
00:43:40,503 --> 00:43:41,664
[SPEAKER_00]: You mustn't lose perspective.

476
00:43:41,764 --> 00:43:53,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if every time you eat, every time you wear clothes, every time you engage with a shelter, or seek shelter, or live in a shelter, you don't lose the context why this environment is as such, and what is its purpose.

477
00:43:53,847 --> 00:44:00,889
[SPEAKER_00]: And if a monk with the right view practices that, a whole layer of defilements will be abandoned, simply on account of that.

478
00:44:02,092 --> 00:44:05,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, defilements to be given by bearing.

479
00:44:06,577 --> 00:44:07,417
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by putting up.

480
00:44:08,298 --> 00:44:16,847
[SPEAKER_00]: So, take monks who, reflected through the source, bears cold, heat, hunger, thirst, bears a touch of flies, mosquitoes, wind, reptiles.

481
00:44:17,247 --> 00:44:19,229
[SPEAKER_00]: He bears rude and unwelcome criticism.

482
00:44:19,410 --> 00:44:23,814
[SPEAKER_00]: He puts up with physical pain, sharp, severe, acute, unpleasant, disagreeable, life-threatening.

483
00:44:24,275 --> 00:44:24,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

484
00:44:25,632 --> 00:44:29,115
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, a degree of discomfort has to be experienced.

485
00:44:29,695 --> 00:44:30,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later it will come.

486
00:44:31,536 --> 00:44:38,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, if there are no flies and mosquitoes and you live in a comfortable environment, well, there is the rude words and unwelcome criticism.

487
00:44:39,222 --> 00:44:40,843
[SPEAKER_00]: People talking badly about you.

488
00:44:40,863 --> 00:44:43,365
[SPEAKER_00]: See, it's on the level of... you have to put up with it.

489
00:44:44,610 --> 00:44:54,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Through the yoniso, through the... just... so when the experience of the bad world is present, you don't try to counter it, defend yourself, show that they're wrong and so on.

490
00:44:54,274 --> 00:44:54,654
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

491
00:44:55,074 --> 00:44:57,215
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to develop yoniso in regard to it.

492
00:44:57,575 --> 00:44:58,595
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you deal with it.

493
00:44:58,695 --> 00:45:02,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So you bear it, so that you can see the source of it.

494
00:45:03,437 --> 00:45:11,340
[SPEAKER_00]: And... but if you just do that to that extent, even if you don't have to seek out the actual physical discomfort, sooner or later they... they will come, obviously.

495
00:45:13,268 --> 00:45:30,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply thinking what somebody said about you, that you didn't like, somebody might say, somebody is saying, you already recognize, oh, see, there is the gratuitous resistance to that discomfort, of words of another, of too much cold, too cold, too hot, insects biting, it's all on the same level.

496
00:45:31,445 --> 00:45:32,426
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do I deal with it?

497
00:45:32,586 --> 00:45:33,087
[SPEAKER_00]: By bearing.

498
00:45:33,107 --> 00:45:34,208
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by not acting out of it,

499
00:45:35,168 --> 00:45:37,749
[SPEAKER_00]: by seeing through it, discerning the source of it.

500
00:45:38,389 --> 00:45:48,271
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when those things are present, you're not attending to who said what, you're allowing that to be, while you're attending to the source of it, correctly, yonisu, manasikāra.

501
00:45:49,191 --> 00:45:57,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you do that, the whole set of defilements, the whole layer of them, will be given up, simply by bearing them, with the right view, not without it.

502
00:45:58,514 --> 00:45:59,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Defilement is given up by avoiding.

503
00:46:01,292 --> 00:46:04,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are the defilements one should give up by avoiding?

504
00:46:04,395 --> 00:46:13,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, take a monk who, reflecting through the source, avoids a wild elephant, a wild horse, a wild ox, a wild dog, a snake, a stump, thorny ground, a pit, cliff, swamp or a sewer.

505
00:46:15,505 --> 00:46:21,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting through the source, he avoids sitting on inappropriate seats, walking in inappropriate neighborhoods, mixing with bad friends.

506
00:46:22,812 --> 00:46:27,478
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is again, somebody who has the right view, recognizes that they still have work to do.

507
00:46:27,659 --> 00:46:28,740
[SPEAKER_00]: They're still not an arahant.

508
00:46:29,261 --> 00:46:39,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's wise for them, even if it's out of fear or distress, to avoid a murderer, avoid where wild elephants might kill you.

509
00:46:40,456 --> 00:46:44,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, there is a degree, oh, I'm afraid, I'm affected by this, I shouldn't be affected by this.

510
00:46:44,863 --> 00:46:45,404
[SPEAKER_00]: That's correct.

511
00:46:45,805 --> 00:46:48,028
[SPEAKER_00]: But the way to not be affected by this, is to become an arahant.

512
00:46:48,068 --> 00:46:51,954
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to become an arahant, you'll need time, because it needs to be developed.

513
00:46:52,014 --> 00:46:55,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order to have time, you need to not be killed, there and then.

514
00:46:56,130 --> 00:47:03,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, avoiding things that will end your life here and now, while at the same time... because at the same time, you recognize that you have not completed the work.

515
00:47:04,357 --> 00:47:12,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me complete the work, free from this imminent danger, and then, when imminent danger comes and you're in arahant, well, you might decide not to move, if there is a wild elephant.

516
00:47:12,204 --> 00:47:12,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

517
00:47:14,366 --> 00:47:21,892
[SPEAKER_00]: The follow-up, the next paragraph that continues, reflecting through the souls, he avoids sitting in inappropriate seats, in appropriate neighborhoods, mixing with bad friends.

518
00:47:21,932 --> 00:47:22,653
[SPEAKER_00]: This refers to

519
00:47:23,846 --> 00:47:26,107
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, even Puthujjana can practice this to a degree.

520
00:47:26,727 --> 00:47:36,971
[SPEAKER_00]: This refers to, like, not putting yourself in the situation or situations that can then compromise your sense restraint and your precepts.

521
00:47:37,552 --> 00:47:39,492
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this particular, this refers to a monk.

522
00:47:39,512 --> 00:47:48,736
[SPEAKER_00]: So, avoiding inappropriate seats would be secluded seats with a female present that then can give rise to either things being said out of lust or done out of lust.

523
00:47:49,296 --> 00:47:50,237
[SPEAKER_00]: They wouldn't have even

524
00:47:50,897 --> 00:48:04,510
[SPEAKER_00]: It would have been possible, no matter even if he wasn't an arahant and free from sensuality, if he didn't even sit in that inappropriate, secluded, hidden seat, private seat, that if he didn't even sit there, no subsequent things would have taken place.

525
00:48:05,564 --> 00:48:12,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody recognizes that I won't even put myself in that position, and then I'll have nothing to deal with it.

526
00:48:12,646 --> 00:48:19,807
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, I will not have broken the rules, compromised the sense restraint, and everything else they could possibly follow for somebody who's not completely free.

527
00:48:20,347 --> 00:48:24,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through rightly reflecting that there is still work to be done, he avoids it.

528
00:48:24,208 --> 00:48:27,869
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, yeah, but, you know, I'm beyond this, I should be able to sit there, and so on.

529
00:48:28,289 --> 00:48:31,930
[SPEAKER_00]: When you complete the work, you might, but then you won't be doing it for those reasons.

530
00:48:31,970 --> 00:48:34,791
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly, those possibilities of breaking the precepts, losing the

531
00:48:38,773 --> 00:48:39,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

532
00:48:39,134 --> 00:48:42,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Walking in inappropriate neighborhoods, mixing with bad friends.

533
00:48:42,556 --> 00:48:47,520
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, bad friends that can put you, inadvertently, in bad situations.

534
00:48:47,540 --> 00:48:55,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like foreseeing those things from the context of yoniso, manasikāra, and the work that still needs to be done, and then avoiding those things once you foresee them.

535
00:48:58,027 --> 00:49:01,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And by doing that, you abandon all the defilements that would come with it.

536
00:49:04,528 --> 00:49:07,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are the defilements the monk should give up by dispelling?

537
00:49:07,890 --> 00:49:16,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, take a monk who, reflecting through the source, he does not tolerate sensual, aversive or cruel thought that has arisen.

538
00:49:16,376 --> 00:49:19,178
[SPEAKER_00]: He gives it up, gets rid of it, eliminates it, obliterates it.

539
00:49:19,639 --> 00:49:21,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that other sutta that I mentioned.

540
00:49:21,180 --> 00:49:24,322
[SPEAKER_00]: He actually explains how one gets rid of it.

541
00:49:24,642 --> 00:49:28,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people usually, like, people come to read the suttas,

542
00:49:30,134 --> 00:49:33,618
[SPEAKER_00]: With preconceived notions already, with some ideas what practice is.

543
00:49:34,439 --> 00:49:39,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, generally, today, you are taught, or whenever these things are present, get rid of it, you know.

544
00:49:39,164 --> 00:49:40,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Essential thought, replace it with this thought.

545
00:49:40,786 --> 00:49:41,847
[SPEAKER_00]: This is replaced with that thought.

546
00:49:41,887 --> 00:49:43,148
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have to deal with it.

547
00:49:43,208 --> 00:49:45,511
[SPEAKER_00]: You actually, you don't have to endure the weight of it.

548
00:49:45,531 --> 00:49:46,652
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to discern it.

549
00:49:47,451 --> 00:49:54,075
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when people read this sutta, it kind of, or suttas like this, I was like, oh, yes, see, the Buddha says you get rid of it, you obliterate it.

550
00:49:54,115 --> 00:49:57,256
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yes, the Buddha says that, but he also says how you do it.

551
00:49:57,777 --> 00:50:02,739
[SPEAKER_00]: And he certainly doesn't say, oh, you get rid of it simply by doing the opposite, or by focusing on your nostrils, and so on.

552
00:50:03,440 --> 00:50:07,482
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, reflecting through the source, you dispel it.

553
00:50:08,860 --> 00:50:16,066
[SPEAKER_00]: We link that sutta, where it clearly describes that for the five hindrances, not aggregates.

554
00:50:16,466 --> 00:50:24,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there's a hindrance of sensuality present, like, if all your concern is to get rid of it, get rid of it, focus away, concentrate on something else, distract yourself from it.

555
00:50:25,173 --> 00:50:32,098
[SPEAKER_00]: If you distract yourself from the hindrance, you will not be able to see what is the necessary basis for the hindrance.

556
00:50:32,158 --> 00:50:35,681
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you discern through the source, through to the source,

557
00:50:36,689 --> 00:50:39,752
[SPEAKER_00]: If your preoccupation is hindrance, get rid of it.

558
00:50:40,553 --> 00:50:48,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Hindrance, look away, focus away, calm down, forget the nostrils, belly, whatever you focus on, just so I don't have to have this pressure in me.

559
00:50:49,260 --> 00:50:58,969
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, again, all this dispelling, all this doesn't tolerate, he gives it up, gets rid of, eliminates, apply only to somebody with sotāpanna.

560
00:50:59,627 --> 00:51:01,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, you need to get the right view.

561
00:51:01,728 --> 00:51:03,849
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to develop yoniso manasikāra, get the right view.

562
00:51:04,270 --> 00:51:08,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you'll be able to see through the central thought, a verse.

563
00:51:08,272 --> 00:51:10,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And by seeing through, you are not tolerating it.

564
00:51:10,930 --> 00:51:23,401
[SPEAKER_00]: By seeing through the nature of the, say, sight that you're preoccupied with, full of lust, seeing through the necessary basis, to that extent, see, you're not now perpetuating gratuitously that sense-objects of pleasure, and so on.

565
00:51:23,421 --> 00:51:29,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with aversion, same with the thought of cruelty, and same, like, again, so, sight, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts.

566
00:51:29,206 --> 00:51:30,247
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same principle.

567
00:51:31,172 --> 00:51:33,674
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he doesn't tolerate these harmful states.

568
00:51:33,734 --> 00:51:40,499
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he does not neglect his work of discerning the necessary source that's simultaneously present.

569
00:51:40,839 --> 00:51:43,561
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you don't tolerate it.

570
00:51:43,841 --> 00:51:51,166
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it says, the one who practices this, no defilements can arise, and this is the call defilements that should be given up by dispelling.

571
00:51:51,246 --> 00:51:56,430
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, we will link the sutta, where the Buddha describes five hindrances,

572
00:51:57,491 --> 00:51:59,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm repeating this because it's very relevant.

573
00:51:59,913 --> 00:52:02,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about when you have a hindrance of ill will, hindrance of sleeping.

574
00:52:02,875 --> 00:52:04,256
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like I'll do the opposite.

575
00:52:04,277 --> 00:52:08,560
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll do the mechanical thing of focusing away of something else, or visualizing thing.

576
00:52:08,580 --> 00:52:08,920
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

577
00:52:09,541 --> 00:52:15,305
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize what pressures you, so that you can discern the necessary basis for that pressure.

578
00:52:15,826 --> 00:52:17,948
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you become unpressured,

579
00:52:18,668 --> 00:52:26,432
[SPEAKER_00]: And that sign of beauty, that for somebody with ayoniso manasikāra means sensual hindrance, remains standing there.

580
00:52:26,732 --> 00:52:27,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the Buddha said.

581
00:52:28,213 --> 00:52:30,474
[SPEAKER_00]: For Tathāgata, there is no desire and lust internally.

582
00:52:31,574 --> 00:52:33,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Beautiful things remain standing in the world.

583
00:52:33,575 --> 00:52:38,578
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that he doesn't perceive what used to be incentive of lust and so on.

584
00:52:39,098 --> 00:52:43,520
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to get there, you need to first get that pressure, recognize the pressure of the hindrance,

585
00:52:44,080 --> 00:52:51,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, try to just immediately, blindly distract yourself, get rid of it, but discern the necessary basis, and that's how you dispel it.

586
00:52:51,524 --> 00:53:05,090
[SPEAKER_00]: I gave the simile many times in the past from the suttas, where it's like a turtle, that the jackal tries to bite its limbs, and the turtle... how does the turtle deal with the māra and temptations and ways of trying to get the turtle to stick its limbs out?

587
00:53:05,690 --> 00:53:11,553
[SPEAKER_00]: By retracting inside sufficiently long enough, then the jackal loses interest, and then you can walk away.

588
00:53:12,301 --> 00:53:13,262
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is it, basically.

589
00:53:13,282 --> 00:53:17,825
[SPEAKER_00]: By perceiving through the pressuring hindrance, that pressure will be taken away from you.

590
00:53:18,645 --> 00:53:27,111
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, once you cultivate that yoniso sufficiently, repeatedly, thoroughly, then even that pressure won't arise anymore.

591
00:53:27,171 --> 00:53:28,992
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would have truly dispelled the hindrances.

592
00:53:29,133 --> 00:53:30,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant is a destroyer of the hindrances.

593
00:53:31,034 --> 00:53:32,075
[SPEAKER_00]: They cannot arise for him.

594
00:53:32,135 --> 00:53:32,875
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what that means.

595
00:53:35,077 --> 00:53:36,598
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

596
00:53:38,579 --> 00:53:41,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we are now to defilements to be given up by developing.

597
00:53:43,317 --> 00:53:56,483
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, again, here a monk, through yoniso manasikāra, through correct context of yoniso manasikāra, develops the awakening, liberating factor of mindfulness, recollection, sati.

598
00:53:57,843 --> 00:54:02,925
[SPEAKER_00]: He develops the factor of discrimination of phenomena, discernment, which is dhammavicaya.

599
00:54:03,986 --> 00:54:11,049
[SPEAKER_00]: He develops the enlightenment factor of effort, virya, energy, zeal, to do the work,

600
00:54:11,850 --> 00:54:18,134
[SPEAKER_00]: He develops the enlightenment factor of pity, which is joy, satisfaction.

601
00:54:19,814 --> 00:54:24,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Passādi, tranquility, and composure, which is samādhi.

602
00:54:26,238 --> 00:54:31,581
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, these things are developed through yoniso manasikāra, by somebody who has the right view.

603
00:54:32,401 --> 00:54:38,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And the final is the equanimity, which rely on withdrawal, dispassion, cessation, and ripen in relinquishment.

604
00:54:41,229 --> 00:54:59,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a monk, through yoniso manasikāra, gives up the defilements by seeing, by restraint, by using, by enduring or bearing, by avoiding, by dispelling,

605
00:55:00,396 --> 00:55:17,749
[SPEAKER_00]: By developing, he's the monk that has surmounted and restrained and withdrawn from all the defilements, he has cut off the craving, untied the fetters, severed the fetters, and by rightly comprehending, the slightest traces of his conceit, basically, are gone.

606
00:55:18,870 --> 00:55:28,718
[SPEAKER_00]: So there is no more even traces of I am, and that flavor of ownership and things being mine, and thus he has made a complete end to suffering.

607
00:55:29,940 --> 00:55:32,821
[SPEAKER_00]: So, satisfied, the monks approved of what the Buddha said.

608
00:55:38,304 --> 00:55:44,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So that, yeah, that kind of concludes the sutta number two.

609
00:55:45,267 --> 00:55:49,068
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't want to go through, like, just a brief recap.

610
00:55:49,089 --> 00:55:56,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, it's like a... for somebody who is not Sotāpanna, obviously, like, the sutta doesn't apply to you.

611
00:55:57,707 --> 00:56:03,270
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said in the previous recording, it's not like you should not try and align yourself with it.

612
00:56:03,830 --> 00:56:16,617
[SPEAKER_00]: But the reason why I keep saying it doesn't apply to you, because it's very easy for somebody with the right view, read the suttas, that are not addressed to them, for them, they don't meet the necessary criteria for it, but you could still make sense out of them.

613
00:56:16,637 --> 00:56:22,680
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you read the suttas and it's like, oh, yeah, okay, I can do enduring, I can do bearing, I can do this, I can do that, I can eat for right reasons.

614
00:56:23,887 --> 00:56:29,730
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, ignore or rather stop thinking that they also need to develop the yoniso manasikāra.

615
00:56:29,830 --> 00:56:32,891
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the necessary basis for all of this to be done rightly.

616
00:56:33,571 --> 00:56:38,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because you make some sense out of it, you stop looking for further sense in it.

617
00:56:39,014 --> 00:56:41,635
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's really the only reason why I keep insisting.

618
00:56:41,675 --> 00:56:42,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

619
00:56:42,515 --> 00:56:44,636
[SPEAKER_00]: The dhamma starts with the right view.

620
00:56:44,656 --> 00:56:46,337
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not a sotāpanna, you do not see the dhamma.

621
00:56:46,917 --> 00:56:49,278
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not sotāpanna, most of the suttas don't apply to you.

622
00:56:49,861 --> 00:56:53,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't see what the practice is, how can you practice different aspects of that practice?

623
00:56:54,645 --> 00:56:55,565
[SPEAKER_00]: It just makes no sense.

624
00:56:56,326 --> 00:56:59,108
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just to put people down or something.

625
00:56:59,608 --> 00:57:05,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like just to prevent from falling into thinking that you know that which you don't.

626
00:57:06,333 --> 00:57:09,915
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can easily fall into that, once you start making sense of all these other things.

627
00:57:09,995 --> 00:57:13,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, you know, satipaṭṭhāna, and all of this, and ānāpānasati.

628
00:57:13,816 --> 00:57:15,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, it's relatable to a degree.

629
00:57:15,617 --> 00:57:20,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see some sense in it, but it's not on the level that it matters.

630
00:57:20,299 --> 00:57:26,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Even this, as we started this sutta, yoniso manasikāra, wise reflection, considerate reflection, whatever people translate it.

631
00:57:28,002 --> 00:57:29,783
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see exactly where that translation came from.

632
00:57:29,863 --> 00:57:31,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Came from making a sense out of it.

633
00:57:32,504 --> 00:57:34,265
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're actually honest and see, but hold on,

634
00:57:35,166 --> 00:57:38,208
[SPEAKER_00]: In other sutras it's said that that is a factor for sotāpatti.

635
00:57:38,748 --> 00:57:45,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, did my wise consideration and instruction of others, is that resulting in sotāpatti when I engage with it?

636
00:57:45,933 --> 00:57:46,773
[SPEAKER_00]: When I engage with it?

637
00:57:47,193 --> 00:57:48,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do I have to keep doing it?

638
00:57:48,054 --> 00:57:52,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have to keep wisely reflecting each time I'm presented with these challenges and so on?

639
00:57:53,077 --> 00:57:58,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then you realize, okay, so maybe it's not... it doesn't match the extent that the Buddha meant by it.

640
00:58:00,360 --> 00:58:03,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have nothing to lose if you take it to mean more.

641
00:58:04,061 --> 00:58:04,801
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only gain.

642
00:58:04,961 --> 00:58:10,223
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if it only meant wise reflection, you were already right by thinking that from the start.

643
00:58:10,923 --> 00:58:24,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it means even something that contains wise reflection, but means even more, such as discerning the necessary basis of the present enduring source of that which you're attending, if you discern that, well, it's a win-win either way.

644
00:58:25,407 --> 00:58:28,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why for as long as there is no right view, one should really

645
00:58:30,388 --> 00:58:44,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Take any sutta that they read with a pinch of salt and not forget the fact that if you were to fully understand any relevant sutta, you know, not the sutta that talks about, like, Buddha instructing householders how to look after their wives and stuff like that.

646
00:58:44,777 --> 00:58:45,397
[SPEAKER_00]: That's irrelevant.

647
00:58:46,098 --> 00:58:47,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly irrelevant for one who wants to practice.

648
00:58:48,479 --> 00:58:59,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you take any sutta that's practically relevant, you you mustn't forget that if I were to understand this sutta on the level that matches, I'll be at least a sotāpanna.

649
00:59:00,446 --> 00:59:04,130
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I were to fully understand it, I would be an arahant like people often did at the end of the sutta.

650
00:59:05,472 --> 00:59:16,625
[SPEAKER_00]: And the point is then to keep that in mind each time, like, wisely reflect, that each time you read the sutta, that should be the threshold.

651
00:59:18,145 --> 00:59:21,168
[SPEAKER_00]: That you need to need to hold in your mind for understanding.

652
00:59:21,228 --> 00:59:32,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you forget about the fact that, well, only Sotāpanna truly understands, begins understanding of the Sutta, then your putujana's understanding of that Sutta will become adequate.

653
00:59:33,179 --> 00:59:36,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And you think, well, I'm not Sotāpanna, but I understood the Sutta, it's good enough.

654
00:59:36,683 --> 00:59:40,006
[SPEAKER_00]: If I understand many other Suttas on this good enough level, it will add up.

655
00:59:40,086 --> 00:59:40,486
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it won't.

656
00:59:41,256 --> 00:59:42,097
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't.

657
00:59:42,137 --> 00:59:48,622
[SPEAKER_00]: It can add up, maybe, in a sense that then it will make you revisit all those suttas and start seeing even more in them.

658
00:59:48,642 --> 00:59:48,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

659
00:59:49,562 --> 00:59:55,106
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, the only time you will start looking for more, is if you don't think you completely understood it.

660
00:59:55,647 --> 01:00:02,932
[SPEAKER_00]: If you take your understanding that you will inevitably have, and if you read anything and you make sense out of it, that's a degree of understanding.

661
01:00:03,613 --> 01:00:10,438
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you... any understanding you have, you take a pinch... with a pinch of salt, read other suttas, then you come back to it, and then see if you can understand even more.

662
01:00:11,465 --> 01:00:12,846
[SPEAKER_00]: And more and more and more.

663
01:00:13,427 --> 01:00:21,795
[SPEAKER_00]: And as I said, if you do that rightly, that's the 50% of the right instruction is being taken rightly.

664
01:00:21,975 --> 01:00:35,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you develop yoniso manasikāra through not attending wrong things, that's why the constant talking about doing things rightly on the right level, sense restraint, withdrawal, celibacy, because it was hard enough to discern yoniso manasikāra back in the day, it's even harder today.

665
01:00:37,309 --> 01:00:49,081
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you pair those two things, and know what to keep looking for, and not fall into a complacent view that because you made sense out of dhamma, thus you understand the dhamma, while at the same time you know you're not a sotāpanna, that's a contradiction in terms.

666
01:00:49,842 --> 01:00:53,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't fall into that view, then you would be keep... well, you would keep pushing forward.

667
01:00:53,786 --> 01:00:55,047
[SPEAKER_00]: You would keep trying to understand.

668
01:00:55,768 --> 01:01:03,952
[SPEAKER_00]: The worst is to think you understood that which you didn't, or you understood that which there still carries more to be understood.

669
01:01:04,092 --> 01:01:06,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yoniso means wise reflection.

670
01:01:06,794 --> 01:01:08,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Fine, I won't think about it anymore.

671
01:01:08,454 --> 01:01:09,355
[SPEAKER_00]: It refers to yoniso.

672
01:01:09,375 --> 01:01:12,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever Buddha says yoniso manasikāra, it means wise reflection.

673
01:01:12,196 --> 01:01:13,417
[SPEAKER_00]: I engage with wise reflection.

674
01:01:13,957 --> 01:01:21,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, you won't look for the immediacy of that term that actually is a factor for arising of the right view.

675
01:01:21,782 --> 01:01:29,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Those who listen to the Buddha and got the right view upon hearing his sentence is because yoniso has been there, waiting, developed.

676
01:01:30,408 --> 01:01:34,631
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't have to go home and wisely reconsider what they read or heard.

677
01:01:35,251 --> 01:01:37,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Just upon hearing it, things slotted in place.

678
01:01:38,834 --> 01:01:43,077
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it needs to be taken on that deeper level of a more immediate level.

679
01:01:43,913 --> 01:01:59,345
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could pretty much take all the other key terms and notions from the suttas on that deeper, more immediate level, that doesn't invalidate your current understanding of it, but it certainly upgrades it.

680
01:02:00,606 --> 01:02:08,732
[SPEAKER_00]: And then again, it would make even more sense how, oh, if somebody truly understands the entire instruction from the Buddha,

681
01:02:09,466 --> 01:02:13,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Back in the day, from the beginning till the end, at the end of the talk, they became an arahant.

682
01:02:13,769 --> 01:02:14,649
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, well, that's why.

683
01:02:14,849 --> 01:02:15,850
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't like some magic.

684
01:02:16,270 --> 01:02:27,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, their mind was already there, thoroughly established with yoniso, imperturbable yoniso, through development of sense-restraint, not giving, not attending, not emphasizing things that shouldn't be emphasized.

685
01:02:27,838 --> 01:02:34,722
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, the Buddha just, just, just described that for them, in terms they should see it, and they saw it, because it was already there, unwavering.

686
01:02:35,182 --> 01:02:39,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind was already developed, tamed, to an unwavering extent, to a sufficient extent.

687
01:02:40,208 --> 01:02:41,790
[SPEAKER_00]: And then arahantship was the result of it.

688
01:02:42,090 --> 01:02:43,732
[SPEAKER_00]: There's like no... nothing magic.

689
01:02:43,772 --> 01:02:50,340
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you could say it's magical experience, but really, they did the work through that diligent and development of those enlightenment factors.

